CONCEPTS = [
('1','fa-globe-asia','Earth as an Interconnected System',r'''<p>Earth is a <strong>system</strong> in which energy and matter move continuously between interacting parts. The Sun is the main external source of energy, while Earth’s hot interior and chemical reactions also drive processes.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Sphere</th><th>What it includes</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Geosphere</td><td>Solid Earth, rocks, soil, landforms and interior</td><td>Deccan Plateau, Thar Desert</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrosphere</td><td>Liquid water</td><td>Oceans, rivers, lakes, groundwater</td></tr><tr><td>Cryosphere</td><td>Frozen water</td><td>Himalayan glaciers, snow, polar ice caps</td></tr><tr><td>Atmosphere</td><td>Gases surrounding Earth</td><td>Air and weather systems</td></tr><tr><td>Biosphere</td><td>Living organisms and habitats</td><td>Forests, farms, plankton, coral reefs</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Systems rule:</strong> a disturbance in one sphere can propagate into several others.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: what makes it a system?</h3>
<p>A system is understood through <strong>components, interactions and changes</strong>. In this chapter the five spheres are the components, while the movement of energy and matter creates the interactions. A change in one component can therefore alter conditions in another component.</p>
<p>For example, snow stored in the cryosphere can later become liquid water in the hydrosphere. That water can support plants in the biosphere, while the plants interact with the atmosphere through transpiration and with the geosphere through their roots and soil.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Concept-check</h4><p>If a question gives a change in only one sphere, do not stop there. Identify the <strong>first direct effect</strong> and then at least one <strong>secondary effect</strong> in another sphere.</p></div>

'''),
('2','fa-snowflake','Sphere-to-Sphere Interactions',r'''<p>The spheres are not isolated. Reduced snowfall in the <strong>cryosphere</strong> can reduce summer lake water in the <strong>hydrosphere</strong>, which can reduce grass available to animals in the <strong>biosphere</strong>. Warmer Arabian Sea water increases evaporation and can alter the southwest monsoon, while warming can accelerate glacier melt and threaten low-lying coastal regions.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Exam chain</h4><p><strong>Change → immediate sphere → linked sphere → ecological/social consequence.</strong> Trace at least two links in system-interaction questions.</p></div>
<h3>Deep cause-and-effect analysis</h3>
<p>The chapter's examples show that Earth-system changes often form a chain rather than a single event. The scientifically useful question is not simply “what happens?” but <strong>“through which process does it happen?”</strong></p>
<table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Initial disturbance</th><th>Process</th><th>Later consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Less snowfall</td><td>Less meltwater entering lakes</td><td>Less water and grass in summer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Warmer Arabian Sea</td><td>More evaporation and altered atmospheric moisture</td><td>Monsoon/rainfall variability</td></tr>
<tr><td>Atmospheric warming</td><td>Faster glacier/ice melting</td><td>Sea-level rise and habitat loss</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Exam skill</h4><p>Use linking words such as <strong>therefore, because, consequently, which in turn</strong>. They make the causal chain explicit.</p></div>

'''),
('3','fa-sun','Solar Radiation and Electromagnetic Spectrum',r'''<p><strong>Solar radiation</strong> is the main energy source for Earth. It reaches Earth as electromagnetic waves, which can travel through vacuum at about <strong>3 × 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>−1</sup></strong>. The electromagnetic spectrum ranges from high-frequency, short-wavelength gamma rays and X-rays to lower-frequency, longer-wavelength radio waves.</p><p>Solar radiation reaching Earth is concentrated mainly in the <strong>UV, visible and infrared</strong> regions. Visible light powers photosynthesis; infrared contributes strongly to surface warming; much harmful UV is absorbed by the ozone layer.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Important distinction</h4><p>Sound waves require a material medium, whereas electromagnetic waves can travel through vacuum.</p></div>
<h3>Deep understanding: wavelength, frequency and energy</h3>
<p>The electromagnetic spectrum is ordered by wavelength and frequency. The chapter describes gamma rays and X-rays as high-frequency/short-wavelength radiation and infrared and radio waves as lower-frequency/longer-wavelength radiation. The important Earth-system idea is that different parts of this spectrum interact differently with the atmosphere and surface.</p>
<p>UV is important because ozone absorbs much of the harmful short-wavelength UV. Visible radiation reaches the surface and supports photosynthesis. The surface then emits infrared radiation, which is central to the greenhouse process.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Three-region memory map</h4><p><strong>UV → ozone protection; Visible → photosynthesis; IR → heat/radiation balance.</strong></p></div>

'''),
('4','fa-radiation','Insolation and Solar Constant',r'''<p><strong>Insolation</strong> is the amount of the Sun’s radiation received by Earth’s surface. The <strong>solar constant</strong> is approximately <strong>1.4 kW m<sup>−2</sup></strong> at the top of the atmosphere for a surface perpendicular to the Sun’s rays. Atmospheric gases, clouds and dust absorb and scatter some energy, so surface insolation is lower; under clear conditions the chapter gives about <strong>1 kW m<sup>−2</sup></strong>.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Numerical pattern</h4><p><strong>E = intensity × area × time.</strong> For 1 kW m<sup>−2</sup> on 1 m<sup>2</sup> for one hour: E = 1000 × 1 × 3600 = <strong>3.6 × 10<sup>6</sup> J</strong>.</p></div>
<h3>Deep understanding: top of atmosphere versus surface</h3>
<p>A common conceptual mistake is treating the solar constant as the energy actually received at the ground. It is defined at the <strong>top of the atmosphere</strong> for a surface perpendicular to the Sun's rays. The atmosphere then absorbs, scatters and reflects part of the incoming radiation.</p>
<h3>Numerical reasoning</h3>
<p>Always convert kilowatts into joules per second when the time is given in seconds: <strong>1 kW = 1000 J s<sup>−1</sup></strong>. Then multiply by area and time. Keep the units visible at every step.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Check your answer</h4><p>Energy must have units of <strong>joule (J)</strong>. If your final unit is W m<sup>−2</sup>, you have found an intensity, not an amount of energy.</p></div>

'''),
('5','fa-adjust','Albedo and Surface Heating',r'''<p><strong>Albedo</strong> is the fraction of incoming solar radiation reflected by a surface. High-albedo surfaces reflect more and generally remain cooler; low-albedo surfaces absorb more and warm more.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Chapter value / tendency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Snow</td><td>0.80–0.90 — very high reflection</td></tr><tr><td>Ice</td><td>0.50–0.70 — high reflection</td></tr><tr><td>Crushed rock</td><td>0.25–0.30</td></tr><tr><td>Black soil / ocean water</td><td>Lower albedo; greater absorption</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Urban heat island:</strong> concrete, asphalt, steel and other built surfaces absorb and retain heat, while vegetation cools surroundings through shade and transpiration.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: absorption controls heating</h3>
<p>When radiation reaches a surface, some is reflected and the remainder can be absorbed. Therefore colour and surface properties influence how quickly a surface warms. Dark roads absorb more solar radiation than light-coloured surfaces, which is why they can become hotter.</p>
<p>Albedo is especially important at the planetary scale because a large reflecting surface sends more incoming solar energy back rather than converting it into surface heating. Snow and ice therefore have a strong cooling influence in the chapter's explanation.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Reasoning pattern</h4><p><strong>Albedo ↑ → reflection ↑ → absorption ↓ → heating tendency ↓.</strong><br><strong>Albedo ↓ → reflection ↓ → absorption ↑ → heating tendency ↑.</strong></p></div>

'''),
('6','fa-earth-americas','Latitude, Earth’s Shape and Uneven Heating',r'''<p>Because Earth is spherical, sunlight strikes different latitudes at different angles. Near the equator, radiation is concentrated over a smaller area; toward the poles it is spread over a larger area. Therefore equatorial regions receive more concentrated heating and polar regions receive less.</p><p>Earth’s shape and axial tilt also contribute to seasonal variation and changing day length. Uneven heating produces temperature differences that help drive <strong>global winds and ocean currents</strong>.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: why geometry changes heating</h3>
<p>The same incoming solar energy is not distributed over the same area at every latitude. Because Earth is spherical, rays are more concentrated near the equatorial region and spread over a larger area toward the poles.</p>
<p>This is why the chapter connects geometry directly to climate: <strong>different energy received per unit area → different temperatures → pressure differences → atmospheric circulation</strong>. Earth’s tilt adds seasonal changes by changing solar conditions and day length during its revolution.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Olympiad-style thinking</h4><p>When comparing two places, first compare the <strong>angle and concentration of incoming radiation</strong>, then infer the likely heating difference. Do not simply say “one place is closer to the Sun.”</p></div>

'''),
('7','fa-cloud-sun','Atmosphere: Composition and Layers',r'''<p>The atmosphere is held by gravity and consists mainly of about <strong>78% nitrogen</strong> and <strong>21% oxygen</strong>, with smaller quantities of argon, carbon dioxide, water vapour and other gases.</p><table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Approx. altitude</th><th>Key feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Troposphere</td><td>0–12 km</td><td>Most weather; temperature decreases with height</td></tr><tr><td>Stratosphere</td><td>12–50 km</td><td>Ozone layer; temperature increases with height</td></tr><tr><td>Mesosphere</td><td>Above stratosphere</td><td>Upper atmospheric layer</td></tr><tr><td>Thermosphere</td><td>Higher</td><td>Upper atmosphere</td></tr><tr><td>Exosphere</td><td>Outermost</td><td>Transitions toward outer space</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Weather → troposphere.</strong> This is a frequent exam trap.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: atmosphere as an energy regulator</h3>
<p>The atmosphere is not just “air we breathe.” In this chapter it is an active part of Earth's energy system. It modifies incoming radiation, contains greenhouse gases that interact with outgoing infrared radiation, and provides the medium in which most weather processes occur.</p>
<p>The vertical temperature structure also matters. In the troposphere, the surface heats the air and temperature generally decreases with height. In the stratosphere, ozone absorption causes temperature to increase with height.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Layer-selection rule</h4><p>If the question mentions <strong>rain, storms, clouds or ordinary weather → troposphere</strong>. If it mentions <strong>ozone and UV absorption → stratosphere</strong>.</p></div>

'''),
('8','fa-shield-alt','Greenhouse Effect and Ozone Layer',r'''<p>The Earth’s surface absorbs sunlight and re-radiates energy as infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases such as <strong>CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub></strong> and water vapour absorb some outgoing heat, helping keep Earth warm enough for life. Excess greenhouse gases intensify this effect and contribute to global warming.</p><p>The <strong>ozone layer</strong> in the stratosphere absorbs harmful UV radiation. CFCs contributed to severe ozone loss over Antarctica. The Montreal Protocol reduced CFC use and the chapter notes that the ozone layer is slowly recovering.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Do not confuse</h4><p><strong>Stratospheric ozone protects from UV.</strong> Ground-level ozone is a harmful air pollutant involved in smog.</p></div>
<h3>Deep understanding: two different radiation problems</h3>
<table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Process</th><th>Radiation involved</th><th>Main atmospheric component</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>UV protection</td><td>Ultraviolet</td><td>Ozone</td><td>Absorbs harmful UV.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Greenhouse effect</td><td>Outgoing infrared</td><td>CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, water vapour</td><td>Absorbs part of outgoing heat.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The natural greenhouse effect is necessary because it helps keep Earth warm enough for life. The concern is <strong>excess greenhouse gases</strong>, which enhance heat retention and contribute to warming.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>High-frequency trap</h4><p>Do not write that CO<sub>2</sub> directly absorbs all incoming sunlight. The chapter's mechanism is that the <strong>surface absorbs solar radiation, re-radiates infrared energy, and greenhouse gases absorb part of that outgoing heat</strong>.</p></div>

'''),
('9','fa-wind','Why Uneven Heating Produces Wind',r'''<p>Wind is air moving from a region of <strong>high pressure toward low pressure</strong>. Uneven solar heating creates temperature differences, which create pressure differences.</p><p><strong>Valley breeze:</strong> during the day, sun-facing slopes heat faster, air rises and cooler valley air moves upslope. <strong>Mountain breeze:</strong> after sunset, slopes cool faster; dense cool air flows downslope.</p><p>At planetary scale, intense equatorial heating creates an equatorial low-pressure belt; sinking air near 30° forms subtropical highs, rising air near 60° forms subpolar lows, and cold sinking air near the poles forms polar highs.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: pressure creates the wind direction</h3>
<p>Heating alone does not describe the complete mechanism. Heating changes air temperature and density; this produces pressure differences, and air moves from higher pressure toward lower pressure.</p>
<p>In a valley during the day, warmed slope air rises and creates relatively lower pressure near the slopes. Cooler valley air therefore moves upslope. At night, cooled dense air over the slopes moves downward.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>One-line mechanism</h4><p><strong>Heating difference → density/pressure difference → air movement.</strong> The time of day tells you which surface is warmer.</p></div>

'''),
('10','fa-compass','Planetary Winds and Earth’s Rotation',r'''<p>Planetary pressure belts establish large-scale wind circulation. Earth’s rotation deflects moving air from a straight path: winds are deflected toward the <strong>right in the Northern Hemisphere</strong> and toward the <strong>left in the Southern Hemisphere</strong>.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Reasoning chain</h4><p><strong>Uneven heating → temperature difference → pressure difference → air movement → rotation deflects the path.</strong></p></div>
<h3>Deep understanding: from local circulation to global circulation</h3>
<p>The same physical principle used for valley and mountain breezes operates on a much larger scale. Unequal heating between equator and poles creates broad pressure belts. Rising and sinking air establish circulation cells, while Earth's rotation curves the moving air.</p>
<table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Dominant vertical motion described</th><th>Pressure character</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Equator</td><td>Warm air rises</td><td>Low pressure</td></tr>
<tr><td>~30° N/S</td><td>Cooled air sinks</td><td>Subtropical high pressure</td></tr>
<tr><td>~60° N/S</td><td>Air rises</td><td>Sub-polar low pressure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Poles</td><td>Cold dense air sinks</td><td>Polar high pressure</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Direction trap</h4><p>Earth's rotation produces <strong>rightward deflection in the Northern Hemisphere</strong> and <strong>leftward deflection in the Southern Hemisphere</strong>.</p></div>

'''),
('11','fa-water','Ocean Currents and Gyres',r'''<p>Ocean currents are continuous movements of large masses of seawater. Surface currents are strongly influenced by planetary winds, while <strong>temperature, salinity, Earth’s rotation and land distribution</strong> also affect circulation.</p><p>Warm equatorial water tends to move toward the poles near the surface; colder, denser water can move back toward the equator at depth. Higher-salinity water is denser and tends to sink. Earth’s rotation helps form large circular patterns called <strong>gyres</strong>: clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and counter-clockwise in the Southern Hemisphere.</p><p>Ocean currents redistribute heat and nutrients, moderating climate and supporting ecosystems.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: why ocean water moves</h3>
<p>Surface and deep ocean movements are connected. Winds can drag surface water, while differences in temperature and salinity alter density. Dense water tends to sink, allowing deeper return flows.</p>
<p>Continents also matter because water cannot continue through solid land; land masses redirect current paths. Earth’s rotation then contributes to large circular gyres.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Integrated chain</h4><p><strong>Uneven heating → winds + temperature differences → ocean movement; salinity → density differences → sinking; rotation + continents → curved/redirected paths.</strong></p></div>
<p>The climate effect is equally important: currents move heat away from warm regions and toward colder regions, reducing temperature contrasts and supporting marine ecosystems through nutrient transport.</p>

'''),
('12','fa-recycle','Biogeochemical Cycles',r'''<p>A <strong>biogeochemical cycle</strong> is the cyclic movement of matter between biotic and abiotic components. Water, carbon, nitrogen and oxygen continuously move among the atmosphere, hydrosphere, geosphere and biosphere.</p><div class="sj-grid"><div class="sj-grid-card"><h4>Why cycles matter</h4><p>They keep essential nutrients available, sustain life, regulate climate and help ecosystems maintain balance.</p></div><div class="sj-grid-card"><h4>Core idea</h4><p>Matter is recycled; energy flows through Earth systems rather than being recycled in the same way.</p></div></div>
<h3>Deep understanding: cycling of matter</h3>
<p>Biogeochemical cycles connect the living and non-living parts of Earth. A nutrient may be present in air, soil, water, rocks or organisms at different stages, but the element itself is transferred rather than permanently consumed.</p>
<p>This is why disruption of a cycle can affect organisms even when the element still exists somewhere in the Earth system. It may be in a form or reservoir that organisms cannot readily use.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Key distinction</h4><p><strong>Energy enters and moves through the Earth system; matter is repeatedly transferred and recycled.</strong></p></div>

'''),
('13','fa-tint','Water Cycle and Climate Change',r'''<p>The water cycle includes <strong>evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, runoff, infiltration and groundwater movement</strong>. Water moves between surface reservoirs, atmosphere and subsurface systems.</p><p>A warmer atmosphere can hold more moisture, contributing to heavier rainfall in some regions and drought in others. Glacier melt can alter river flow and contribute to long-term sea-level rise. Intense rainfall can increase runoff and soil erosion while reducing infiltration and groundwater recharge.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Sphere linkage</h4><p>Cryosphere ↔ hydrosphere ↔ atmosphere ↔ geosphere ↔ biosphere.</p></div>
<h3>Deep understanding: reservoirs and transfers</h3>
<p>The water cycle should be understood as movement between <strong>reservoirs</strong>, not merely as “rain.” Oceans, lakes, rivers, groundwater, glaciers, the atmosphere and living organisms can all store or transfer water.</p>
<p>Evaporation and transpiration add water vapour; condensation forms clouds; precipitation returns water to the surface; runoff moves it over land; infiltration transfers it into soil and rocks.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Climate reasoning</h4><p>If intense rainfall increases runoff but decreases infiltration, the result can be <strong>more soil erosion + less groundwater recharge</strong>. This explains why heavy rain does not automatically mean better water availability.</p></div>

'''),
('14','fa-leaf','Carbon Cycle',r'''<p>Carbon is present in proteins, carbohydrates, fats and DNA. It cycles among the atmosphere as CO<sub>2</sub>, biosphere, geosphere and hydrosphere.</p><p><strong>Fast cycle:</strong> plants use atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> in photosynthesis; respiration and decomposition return carbon to the atmosphere. <strong>Slow cycle:</strong> buried organic matter can become fossil fuels over millions of years; combustion releases carbon rapidly as CO<sub>2</sub>.</p><p>Oceans absorb atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub>, forming dissolved carbonate and bicarbonate ions; phytoplankton use these in photosynthesis and some marine organisms use them to form shells.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: carbon moves at very different speeds</h3>
<p>The carbon cycle contains both rapid biological transfers and very slow geological storage. This time-scale difference is essential. Plants can move atmospheric carbon into organic matter within a short period, while fossil-fuel formation represents storage over millions of years.</p>
<p>Human combustion reverses this time scale: carbon stored over geological periods can be transferred to the atmosphere rapidly as CO<sub>2</sub>.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>High-value comparison</h4><p><strong>Photosynthesis stores atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> in organic matter; respiration, decomposition and combustion return carbon toward the atmosphere.</strong></p></div>

'''),
('15','fa-atom','Nitrogen Cycle',r'''<p>Nitrogen is essential for proteins and nucleic acids. Although atmospheric N<sub>2</sub> is abundant, it is relatively unreactive and cannot be directly used by plants and animals.</p><ol><li><strong>Nitrogen fixation:</strong> bacteria such as Rhizobium and Azotobacter convert atmospheric nitrogen into usable compounds.</li><li><strong>Assimilation:</strong> plants absorb nitrogen compounds; animals obtain nitrogen through food.</li><li><strong>Ammonification:</strong> decomposers return nitrogen compounds such as ammonia to soil.</li><li><strong>Nitrification:</strong> bacteria convert ammonia → nitrite → nitrate.</li><li><strong>Denitrification:</strong> bacteria such as Pseudomonas convert some nitrates back to atmospheric nitrogen.</li></ol><p>The chapter also describes the Haber–Bosch process, which industrially fixes nitrogen for fertiliser production.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: why nitrogen fixation is necessary</h3>
<p>The atmosphere contains abundant N<sub>2</sub>, but abundance does not mean direct biological availability. Because N<sub>2</sub> is relatively non-reactive, it must first be converted into compounds that organisms can use.</p>
<p>The cycle is therefore a sequence of chemical and biological transformations rather than a simple movement of nitrogen gas.</p>
<table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Process</th><th>What to remember</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Fixation</td><td>Atmospheric N<sub>2</sub> → usable nitrogen compounds.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assimilation</td><td>Plants take up nitrogen; animals obtain it through food.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ammonification</td><td>Decomposition returns nitrogen as ammonia/related compounds.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nitrification</td><td>Ammonia → nitrite → nitrate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Denitrification</td><td>Nitrate → atmospheric nitrogen.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

'''),
('16','fa-lungs','Oxygen Cycle',r'''<p>Oxygen forms about <strong>21%</strong> of Earth’s atmosphere. Respiration and combustion consume oxygen and release CO<sub>2</sub>; photosynthesis uses sunlight, water and CO<sub>2</sub> to form glucose and release O<sub>2</sub>.</p><div class="sj-detail"><h4>Balance</h4><p><strong>Photosynthesis restores O<sub>2</sub>; respiration + combustion consume O<sub>2</sub>.</strong></p></div>
<h3>Deep understanding: oxygen is linked to carbon</h3>
<p>The oxygen cycle is not an isolated cycle. Photosynthesis simultaneously <strong>uses CO<sub>2</sub> and releases O<sub>2</sub></strong>, while respiration does the reverse overall by consuming O<sub>2</sub> and releasing CO<sub>2</sub>. Combustion also consumes oxygen and releases CO<sub>2</sub>.</p>
<p>Therefore questions about deforestation can legitimately ask about both carbon and oxygen: fewer plants means less photosynthetic restoration of O<sub>2</sub> and less removal of atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub>.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Memory pair</h4><p><strong>Photosynthesis: O<sub>2</sub> out, CO<sub>2</sub> in.</strong><br><strong>Respiration/combustion: O<sub>2</sub> in, CO<sub>2</sub> out.</strong></p></div>

'''),
('17','fa-industry','Human Disruption of Earth’s Processes',r'''<p>Burning fossil fuels and deforestation increase atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub>. Excess CO<sub>2</sub> also increases ocean absorption and can make seawater more acidic, threatening plankton and coral reefs.</p><p>Excess fertiliser adds nitrogen to rivers and lakes, causing <strong>eutrophication</strong>: algal blooms grow rapidly, oxygen is depleted and fish can die. Deforestation reduces photosynthesis and transpiration, can alter albedo and rainfall, increases soil erosion and destroys habitats.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: one action can disturb multiple cycles</h3>
<p>Human impacts become easier to understand when classified by the Earth-system process they interrupt.</p>
<table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Human action</th><th>Immediate system change</th><th>Wider consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Fossil-fuel burning</td><td>More atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub></td><td>Enhanced greenhouse warming and climate impacts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deforestation</td><td>Less photosynthesis/transpiration</td><td>Carbon, water, soil, climate and habitat changes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fertiliser overuse</td><td>Excess nitrogen enters water</td><td>Eutrophication and oxygen depletion.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vehicle emissions</td><td>Pollutants react with sunlight</td><td>Smog and harmful ground-level ozone.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Exam strategy</h4><p>Whenever a question asks for “other consequences,” move across the spheres instead of repeating the same type of effect.</p></div>

'''),
('18','fa-smog','Air Pollution, Smog and Ozone',r'''<p>Vehicular emissions can react with sunlight to form ground-level smog and ground-level ozone, which is harmful. This must be distinguished from stratospheric ozone, which absorbs UV and protects life.</p><p>The chapter connects local actions with international cooperation: the Montreal Protocol is presented as a successful example of ozone protection, while the Kyoto Protocol and Paris Agreement are noted as less successful in reducing CO<sub>2</sub> emissions.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: location changes the meaning of ozone</h3>
<p>The word “ozone” alone is not enough to decide whether an effect is protective or harmful. The chapter makes a location-based distinction.</p>
<table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Ozone</th><th>Where?</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Protective ozone</td><td>Stratosphere</td><td>Absorbs harmful UV radiation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ground-level ozone</td><td>Near Earth’s surface</td><td>Forms through sunlight-driven reactions involving pollutants and is harmful to health.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Vehicular emissions therefore connect atmospheric chemistry with human health. This is another example of a local process becoming part of the broader Earth-system discussion.</p>

'''),
('19','fa-solar-panel','Solutions and Sustainable Choices',r'''<p>Restoring Earth-system balance requires conserving energy and resources, using renewable energy such as solar and wind, planting trees, saving water, sustainable farming, and reducing, reusing and recycling materials.</p><p><strong>Systems thinking:</strong> a good solution should reduce pressure on more than one sphere or cycle where possible—for example, renewable energy can reduce fossil-fuel emissions and help slow disruption of the carbon cycle.</p>
<h3>Deep understanding: sustainability as systems management</h3>
<p>The solutions listed in the chapter are connected to the same processes that human activity disrupts. Renewable energy can reduce dependence on fossil fuels; tree planting supports carbon uptake and ecosystems; water conservation reduces pressure on the hydrosphere; sustainable farming can reduce nutrient pollution.</p>
<p>Individual actions such as reducing waste, reusing and recycling are presented as part of a larger collective response. The chapter also highlights <strong>Mission LiFE</strong> as an India-led global initiative encouraging environmentally mindful lifestyles.</p>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Best conceptual answer</h4><p>A sustainable solution should be explained by stating <strong>which pressure it reduces</strong> and <strong>which Earth-system process benefits</strong>.</p></div>

'''),
('20','fa-brain','High-Level Exam Reasoning and Connections',r'''<p>For long-answer questions, connect processes rather than listing facts.</p><div class="sj-grid"><div class="sj-grid-card"><h4>Climate chain</h4><p>Solar radiation → uneven heating → pressure differences → winds → ocean currents → heat redistribution → climate.</p></div><div class="sj-grid-card"><h4>Human-impact chain</h4><p>Fossil fuels/deforestation → CO<sub>2</sub> rise → greenhouse warming → ice/water/ecosystem changes.</p></div><div class="sj-grid-card"><h4>Nutrient chain</h4><p>Biogeochemical cycles → nutrient availability → organisms → decomposition → return to environment.</p></div><div class="sj-grid-card"><h4>Water chain</h4><p>Warming → more atmospheric moisture → changed rainfall/runoff → erosion/recharge changes → agriculture and ecosystems affected.</p></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing rule:</strong> state the cause, explain the mechanism, identify the affected sphere or cycle, then state the consequence.</p>
<h3>Deep reasoning toolkit</h3>
<p>Chapter 13 questions often look different on the surface but can be solved using a small number of process chains.</p>
<table class="sj-table"><thead><tr><th>Question type</th><th>Reasoning chain</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Uneven heating</td><td>Sun → unequal energy per unit area → temperature difference → pressure difference.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind</td><td>Pressure difference → air movement → rotation deflects path.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ocean current</td><td>Wind + temperature + salinity + rotation + land → water circulation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Climate change</td><td>Warming → ice/water/atmosphere changes → ecosystem and human impacts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carbon</td><td>Photosynthesis → food web → respiration/decomposition; long-term burial → fossil fuel → combustion.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>Fixation → assimilation → ammonification → nitrification → denitrification.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Human impact</td><td>Human activity → disrupted cycle/process → sphere interaction → ecological/social consequence.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="sj-detail"><h4>Four-step long-answer method</h4><p><strong>1. Define</strong> the key term. <strong>2. Explain</strong> the mechanism in sequence. <strong>3. Connect</strong> it to another sphere/cycle. <strong>4. Conclude</strong> with the consequence or example from the chapter.</p></div>

''')]
_engine.CONCEPTS=CONCEPTS

_engine.NCERT=[
 nq(1,'Choose the most appropriate option to describe the role of biogeochemical cycles in an ecosystem.','<p><strong>Answer: (ii) To recycle essential nutrients between biotic and abiotic components.</strong></p>','1',['(i) To provide food directly to all organisms.','(ii) To recycle essential nutrients between biotic and abiotic components.','(iii) To create new elements for use by living things.','(iv) To remove pollutants and toxins from the organism.']),
 nq(2,'Which of the following is primarily responsible for warming of the Earth?','<p><strong>Answer: (iii) The Earth’s surface absorbs solar radiation, which is then re-radiated and trapped by greenhouse gases.</strong></p>','1',['(i) Solar radiation is immediately absorbed by carbon dioxide, which then releases it as heat.','(ii) The atmosphere’s tiny particles absorb incoming solar radiation, which directly heats the Earth.','(iii) The Earth’s surface absorbs solar radiation, which is then re-radiated and trapped by greenhouse gases.','(iv) The Earth’s environment is heated only by the solar radiation reflected by the clouds.']),
 nq(3,'Explain how climate change affects the water cycle. Illustrate with examples.','<p>Climate change can make the atmosphere hold more moisture, causing heavier rainfall in some places and drought in others. Glacier melting changes river water and contributes to long-term sea-level rise. Intense rainfall can increase runoff and soil erosion while reducing infiltration and groundwater recharge.</p>','3'),
 nq(4,'Describe how albedo affects the Earth’s surface temperature and its climate.','<p>Albedo is the fraction of incoming solar radiation reflected by a surface. High-albedo surfaces reflect more and absorb less, tending to remain cooler; low-albedo surfaces absorb more and warm more. Therefore changes in albedo can influence surface temperature and climate.</p>','3'),
 nq(5,'How are mountain and valley breezes formed? Suppose there are two mountains, one covered with grass and another covered with barren rocks; would the temperature of the two mountain breezes be different? If so, how?','<p>During the day, slopes heat and warm air rises, producing a valley breeze as cooler valley air moves upslope. After sunset, slopes cool and denser air flows downslope as a mountain breeze. Grass and barren rock surfaces can heat and cool differently because of their surface properties, so the resulting breeze temperatures can differ.</p>','4'),
 nq(6,'You have witnessed weather phenomena, such as winds, storms, rainfall, etc. Which atmospheric layer is mainly responsible for such phenomena and what is the primary reason for its occurrence?','<p><strong>Troposphere.</strong> Most weather occurs there because it is heated from the Earth’s surface. Warm air rises, producing convection, pressure differences and atmospheric circulation.</p>','3'),
 nq(7,'Explain the processes involved in the nitrogen cycle. How would life on Earth be affected if nitrogen were not cycled?','<p>The processes are nitrogen fixation, assimilation, ammonification, nitrification and denitrification. Without cycling, usable nitrogen would not be continually replenished, disrupting plant growth, food webs and ecosystem productivity.</p>','4'),
 nq(8,'What are the impacts of deforestation on the Earth’s oxygen and carbon cycles? What are the other consequences of deforestation?','<p>Deforestation reduces photosynthesis, so less CO<sub>2</sub> is removed and less O<sub>2</sub> is restored. It can release stored carbon and weaken carbon sinks. Other consequences include reduced transpiration, altered rainfall, changed albedo, increased soil erosion and habitat loss.</p>','4'),
 nq(9,'Explain with suitable diagram the path that carbon takes to go back to the atmosphere. You may start from plants using CO2 from the atmosphere.','<p>Plants take in CO<sub>2</sub> by photosynthesis. Carbon moves through food chains. Respiration and decomposition return CO<sub>2</sub> to the atmosphere. Over long periods, buried carbon can become fossil fuels; combustion returns it rapidly as CO<sub>2</sub>.</p>','4'),
 nq(10,'Why is an excess of CO2 in the atmosphere considered undesirable even though it is required by plants?','<p>CO<sub>2</sub> is necessary for photosynthesis and the natural greenhouse effect, but excess CO<sub>2</sub> intensifies greenhouse warming, contributing to global warming, ice melt, sea-level rise and changing rainfall.</p>','3'),
 nq(11,'How is heat lost from the surface of the Earth? What is its significance?','<p>The surface re-radiates energy mainly as infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases absorb some outgoing heat. This is significant because it keeps Earth warm enough for life; excess greenhouse gases enhance heat retention.</p>','3'),
 nq(12,'If the Earth were a flat disc instead of a sphere, how would the patterns of solar radiation and temperature be different?','<p>Earth’s spherical shape makes sunlight strike different latitudes at different angles and spread over different areas. A flat disc would have a different distribution of solar radiation, so the present equator-to-pole temperature pattern and associated wind and climate patterns would be altered.</p>','3'),
 nq(13,'Suppose there is a rise in atmospheric temperature on Earth. How would this affect the cryosphere, hydrosphere and biosphere?','<p>Higher temperature can accelerate glacier and polar-ice melting in the cryosphere. Meltwater changes the hydrosphere and contributes to long-term sea-level rise. Changes in temperature, water availability, rainfall and habitats affect the biosphere.</p>','3'),
 nq(14,'Explain how the Earth’s atmosphere helps in maintaining a suitable temperature for life to survive on the Earth.','<p>The atmosphere absorbs part of incoming radiation, while greenhouse gases absorb some outgoing infrared radiation. The natural greenhouse effect prevents Earth from becoming too cold. Excess greenhouse gases, however, cause excessive warming.</p>','3'),
 nq(15,'Describe the interrelationship between different spheres of the Earth. Illustrate with example how these spheres function in a delicate balance.','<p>The geosphere, hydrosphere, cryosphere, atmosphere and biosphere exchange matter and energy. For example, reduced snowfall can lower summer lake levels, reducing grass available to animals; warming can accelerate glacier melt, alter rainfall and affect habitats.</p>','4')]

